<% content_for :title, "About" %>
<%# RUBRIC 2.1 — About page with the data source details from 1.1 (+ ERD from 1.2) %>
<div class="row">
  <div class="col-lg-8">
    <p class="label mb-2">Colophon</p>
    <h1 class="mb-4">About Shade Finder</h1>
    <p>Shade Finder is a Ruby on Rails app for browsing eye makeup products — eyeshadows and eyeliners —
      with their brands, categories, tags, colour shades and customer reviews.</p>

    <%# RUBRIC 1.1 — Describe datasets %>
    <h2 class="mt-5 mb-3">Data sources</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Makeup API — eyeshadow endpoint</strong>
        (<a href="https://makeup-api.herokuapp.com/api/v1/products.json?product_type=eyeshadow">products.json?product_type=eyeshadow</a>).
        A JSON array of products. Each product has a brand, name, price, rating, category (palette, cream, pencil…),
        description, image links, a <code>tag_list</code> array (Vegan, Organic, EWG Verified…) and a
        <code>product_colors</code> array of <code>{ hex_value, colour_name }</code> objects.</li>
      <li><strong>Makeup API — eyeliner endpoint</strong>
        (<a href="https://makeup-api.herokuapp.com/api/v1/products.json?product_type=eyeliner">products.json?product_type=eyeliner</a>).
        Same structure as the eyeshadow endpoint, with categories like liquid, pencil and gel.</li>
      <li><strong>Faker gem</strong> — fake customer reviews (name, 1–5 stars, text, date) attached to every product.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-5 mb-3">How the data is structured and related</h2>
    <p>Each nested part of the API JSON becomes its own table:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <code>brand</code> string becomes a <strong>brands</strong> row — a brand has many products.</li>
      <li>The <code>category</code> string becomes a <strong>categories</strong> row — a category has many products
        (products with no category go to “Uncategorized”).</li>
      <li>Each <code>tag_list</code> entry becomes a <strong>tags</strong> row, joined to products through
        <strong>product_tags</strong> (many-to-many).</li>
      <li>Each unique <code>hex_value</code> becomes a <strong>colors</strong> row, joined to products through
        <strong>product_colors</strong>, which also stores the brand's <code>colour_name</code> (many-to-many).
        The same hex shade can appear in products from different brands.</li>
      <li>The Faker <strong>reviews</strong> belong to a product — a product has many reviews.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both API endpoints share the same brands, categories, tags and colors tables, so the two datasets are
      integrated: for example, a brand page shows that brand's eyeshadows and eyeliners together.</p>
  </div>
</div>

